WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:05.120
Every response a language model
streams to you comes from two different jobs.

2
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:09.320
Learn to tell them apart, and you can
say which job is making a user wait.

3
00:00:09.487 --> 00:00:10.807
Two terms first.

4
00:00:10.947 --> 00:00:14.747
A token is the unit of
text a model reads and writes.

5
00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:18.987
A forward pass is one trip of data
through every layer of the model.

6
00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.520
The first job is prefill.

7
00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:26.540
The model takes every token of the
prompt in parallel, in one forward pass.

8
00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.560
Along the way, it stores each
token's keys and values in the KV cache.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.320
Prefill ends with the first output token.

10
00:00:34.502 --> 00:00:35.932
The second job is decode.

11
00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:39.092
Each forward pass produces one new token.

12
00:00:39.092 --> 00:00:44.612
That token joins the sequence, its keys
and values join the cache, and the loop runs

13
00:00:44.612 --> 00:00:46.332
again until the model stops.

14
00:00:46.669 --> 00:00:48.789
That split tells you where the time goes.

15
00:00:48.889 --> 00:00:50.639
Prefill sets time to first token.

16
00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:52.739
Decode sets tokens per second.

17
00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:54.748
Pause and sort these two requests.

18
00:00:54.878 --> 00:01:00.008
Request A sends a 50-token
question and asks for a long essay.

19
00:01:00.078 --> 00:01:06.038
Request B sends a 3,000-token
contract and asks for a one-line summary.

20
00:01:06.198 --> 00:01:09.868
Which one spends most of its time
in prefill, and which in decode?
